// # FLOOR — Builder Contract
//
// Logline: Tell builders raising the floor how to work: front doors
//          first, services for speed, CI for releases, qa with every
//          fix, docs in the same commit, one repo per commit.
//
// Setup: Owner-maintained (ALLCAPS name per RULES.oot §1.19). Read the repo
//        ANCHOR.oo onboarding before this file. This file serves builders
//        working ON openOODA, not users. User docs live elsewhere.
//
// Beats:
//   1. Remember the fence: floor vs factory.
//   2. Reach the front doors before touching code.
//   3. Use MCP, LSP, and blackbox to cut tokens.
//   4. Commit and push everything; CI versions and releases.
//   5. Grow local qa with every fix.
//   6. Think: zero trust, first principles, systems, alignment, ocean.
//   7. Update docs and the task source in the same commit.
//   8. Pass the done checklist, or stop and ask.
//   9. Respect repo lines: one repo, one commit, its own version.

// 1. The fence.
// Builders raise the floor. Users get the factory later.
// We are builders. Nothing in this file serves end users.
// Do not write user docs here. Do not build user features here.
// The floor is done when the factory can run without us.

// 2. Front doors first.
// Every repo opens with ANCHOR.oo. Read it first.
// Follow its beats: rules, north star, RFCs, practices, in order.
// Never edit a file you have not re-read from disk this turn.
// Never rely on cached file contents across turns.

// 3. Services for speed.
// Do not load whole files when a service call answers the question.
// MCP tools read orientation, boards, and RFC text. Use them
// instead of pasting governance files into context.
// LSP finds definitions. Use it instead of grepping the tree.
// Blackbox autopsy diagnoses crashes. Use it on the first failure,
// not after three turns of guessing.
// Rule of thumb: if you are about to paste a full file to find
// one fact, stop and call the service instead.
// Fresh `git pull` before you trust any local binary. The
// repo-committed toolchain is the source of truth. Check it with:
//   git log --oneline -1 -- oodac/bin/oodac
// Never use an unversioned install path. It rots silently.

// 4. Environment.
// Export these before running the toolchain:
//   OODA_COMPILER  — full path to oodac. No PATH hunt.
//   OODA_FS_READDIR — workspace root the caps may read.
//   OODA_FS_WRITEDIR — workspace root the caps may write.
//   OODA_NO_JAIL=1 — only in containers where Landlock cannot apply.
// Read and write roots match CI. CI sets both to its workspace.
// The compiler writes token artifacts under .ooda-cache/ooda-tmp/.
// Create that directory if `check` reports an artifact write failure.
// Suites resolve board, fixture, and probe paths from the working
// directory. Run from the polyrepo root unless the suite documents
// otherwise. A suite that fails only under your CWD is your harness
// error, not a code defect. Reproduce CI exactly before blaming code.

// 5. Commit and push.
// Commit and push all changes. Every completed turn ships.
// Name every file you stage. Never use a broad add on a dirty tree.
// CI derives versions from history and cuts releases per push.
// Never hand-version. Never hand-tag. Never hand-release.
// CI builds all binaries and runs all script work. The local
// machine is for editing and fast checks only.
// Never commit files you did not change and cannot explain.
// Never rewrite history: no amend, rebase, reset, or force-push
// unless explicitly asked for that exact operation.

// 6. Local qa.
// Every fix lands with a hostile proof driver next to it, in qa/.
// Falsify, never confirm: oversized, invalid, empty, and hostile
// inputs first. A test that always passes proves nothing.
// Run each proof twice in fresh processes. Both runs must pass
// identically. That is the double-run law.
// A check built from your own assumption proves nothing. Verify
// against the repo's own tests, not your own scratch script.

// 7. How to think.
// Zero trust: assume every unverified file, test, and claim is
// defective until a hostile probe says otherwise.
// First principles: derive from hardware specs, KATs, and RFCs.
// Never copy unverified code. Never wrap an incomplete stub.
// Systems thinking: layers stay separate, topology stays still,
// invariants hold across turns. Fix the base before the tower.
// Functional alignment: every file earns one responsibility.
// Delete what earns nothing. Subtraction beats addition.
// Blue ocean: remove code, privileges, and steps. Do not add
// machinery where deletion solves the problem.

// 8. Docs and tasks in the same commit.
// Every repo keeps docs/. Update them in the same commit as the
// behavior change. A behavior change with no docs change is
// incomplete. Stale docs are a defect, not entropy.
// If the repo keeps a task list, update it in the same commit.
// A finished task with no list update is incomplete.
// If no task is assigned and none is derivable in one reading,
// stop and ask. Circling for work is a defect. Never invent
// roadmap work to stay busy.

// 9. Done checklist.
// Ship only when every line holds, or stop and ask:
//   1. Hostile proofs pass twice, identically.
//   2. That repo's docs/ updated.
//   3. Task source updated, or none exists.
//   4. Only your files staged.
//   5. Committed and pushed; CI is running.

// 10. Repo lines.
// One repo, one commit. Never bundle changes across repos.
// Each repo versions and releases from its own history alone.
// Read across repos freely. Write in exactly one.
// Governance carries no code. Drivers carry no stdlib. Compiler
// internals never leak into services. If a fix seems to need two
// repos at once, stop. That is a design smell. Surface it.
